# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cutover credentials — copy to cutover.env and fill in
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# cutover.env is gitignored. Keep it that way, and DELETE IT when the migration
# is done: a service-role key bypasses every RLS policy in its project and is
# the most powerful credential either database has.
#
# They live in a file rather than in the shell so a key is never typed into a
# terminal that keeps history and never sits in an environment other processes
# can read. This file has a lifetime of one migration.
#
# ─── Which credential, and why not the other one ────────────────────────────
# The SERVICE-ROLE key (Project Settings -> API Keys -> service_role, or a
# `sb_secret_...` key). NOT the anon/publishable key.
#
# That distinction is the whole thing. The publishable key is public by design
# — it is compiled into the website's JavaScript — and RLS refuses it on every
# table involved here. Used by mistake it would return an empty list for all
# ten tables, and "no rows anywhere" is indistinguishable from "there was
# nothing to migrate". migrate-data.py refuses a key that does not look like a
# service-role key for exactly that reason.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─── The OLD, shared project: TanCerca's, where Unchained's rows are today ───
# Read-only in this migration. Only the `export` phase touches it, and the
# decommission step is separate, later, and deliberately manual.
TANCERCA_URL='https://jkjsjojwlrzpcpggqgws.supabase.co'
TANCERCA_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=''

# ─── Unchained Business's own project ────────────────────────────────────────
UNCHAINED_URL='https://sgyejgsaqknbmnzqfbul.supabase.co'
UNCHAINED_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=''

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The shell scripts (export-from-tancerca.sh / import-into-unchained.sh) use
# pg_dump and psql instead, and need DATABASE CONNECTION STRINGS rather than
# API keys. They are the better tool on a machine that has those binaries; this
# one does not, so migrate-data.py is the path in use. If you ever set the
# client tools up, the variables they want are:
#
#   TANCERCA_DB_URL='postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@<host>:5432/postgres'
#   UNCHAINED_DB_URL='postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@<host>:5432/postgres'
#
# Direct connection on port 5432, not the transaction pooler on 6543, and
# percent-encode the password if it contains @ : / ? # or &.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
